Shell Scripting Basics Simplified 🚀

Over the past few days, I’ve been diving deeper into Shell Scripting 🖥️ — one of the most powerful skills for anyone in DevOps, Linux, or Production Support. Here’s a crisp summary of what I’ve learned so far:

🔹 Variables – Different ways to declare and use (inside script, as arguments, read command, ENV, and special variables).
🔹 Datatypes – Arrays, indexes, string handling.
🔹 Conditions – Writing clear logic (if/else) and thinking in algorithms before jumping into code.
🔹 Exit Status – Understanding success (0) and failure (1–127), and handling errors gracefully.
🔹 Functions – Reusable, modular blocks that improve readability and performance.
🔹 Logging & Colors – Making script outputs meaningful (Green for Success ✅, Red for Failure ❌, Yellow for Skip ⚠️).
🔹 Redirections – Managing outputs and errors effectively.

💡 What I loved most: It’s not just about writing scripts, but about writing them efficiently — clean, reusable, and with proper error handling.

👉 If you’re starting your DevOps or Linux journey, mastering these fundamentals will make automation feel like second nature.
